Why vendored:
- The crates.io index has no fixed release newer than 0.23.5.
- Upstream PR https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-c-sharp/pull/442
  (head 8f21f2f5a7b6bc568202c738d17a6f47d65e9e09, still open) is the
  relevant async contextual-identifier repair, so no published dependency
  can provide this fix yet.

Local grammar repair, applied to the v0.23.5 grammar:
- Incorporate PR #442's four grammar changes: add `async` to
  `_reserved_identifier`; prefer the `async`/`static` modifier path for local
  functions; and raise the modifier precedence in lambda and anonymous-method
  initializers so genuine async constructs remain structured.
- Add `await` to `_reserved_identifier` as the matching contextual-keyword
  repair. The existing `await_expression` literal remains available where
  `await` is an operator; no source rewriting or error recovery is added.
- The prioritized local-function path accepts either `async` or `static` as
  its first modifier, followed by the existing modifier repeat. This preserves
  plain `static`, `static async`, `async static`, and plain `async` local
  functions instead of falling through to the lambda interpretation.

Generated artifacts (`src/grammar.json`, `src/node-types.json`, and
`src/parser.c`) were regenerated from this exact grammar with:

    npx --yes tree-sitter-cli@0.25.10 generate

Run that command from `vendor/tree-sitter-c-sharp` after changing grammar.js,
then restore `src/tree_sitter/array.h` byte-for-byte from the v0.23.5 base.
The base array header contains a strict-aliasing fix; tree-sitter CLI
regeneration replaces it with whole-struct casts, so the header is
intentionally not regenerated.
Suggested targeted behavior checks (outside the vendored package) use the
existing valid contextual-identifier source
`.oracle/sonar/projects/oracle-cs/s2306_bad.cs` and malformed C# inputs such as
`class C { private int async; private int Get() { int await = ; return await; } }`.
The valid source must have no parse error; malformed sources must retain
`root.has_error()` so callers fail closed.
The broader regression matrix should also cover:
- contextual parameter: `void M(bool async) { Use(async); }`
- contextual field/local/return uses: `private int async; int await = async; return await;`
- genuine modifier/operator forms: `async Task Run() => await Work();`,
  `static async Task A() => await Work();`,
  `async static Task B() => await Work();`,
  `var f = async () => await Work();`, and `async delegate { await Work(); }`
- combined local-function control: `void L() { static async Task A() => await Work(); async static Task B() => await Work(); }`;
  both local functions must retain modifier nodes and nested
  `await_expression` nodes with no `ERROR`.
- ordinary static local control: `void M() { static int A() => 1; }` must
  produce a local-function node with its `static` modifier and no `ERROR`.
- malformed negatives: `await (Work();` and a declaration with a missing initializer.
These cases must keep modifier nodes and `await_expression` structure where
the keyword is an operator, while only valid identifier positions use the
`identifier` node.
